<section>
    <h2>Which tool, when?</h2>
    <table class="compare" style="font-size: 0.5em;">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="width: 32%">To test…</th>
                <th>Use</th>
                <th style="width: 22%">Runs</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr class="fragment">
                <td>The normal flow with a third-party API</td>
                <td><code>#[UseRecord]</code>, replayed from the <code>.har</code> file</td>
                <td>Every commit</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="fragment">
                <td>Errors, timeouts, rare edge cases</td>
                <td><code>MockHttpClient</code> + <code>MockResponse</code></td>
                <td>Every commit</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="fragment">
                <td>The request your code sends</td>
                <td>Matching on replay, or a <code>MockHttpClient</code> callback</td>
                <td>Every commit</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="fragment">
                <td>The API has not changed</td>
                <td><code>passthrough</code> in dev, then <code>record</code> and review the diff</td>
                <td>Regularly, by hand</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="fragment callout" style="font-size: 0.6em; margin-top: 0.6em;">
        <b>Offline on every commit, real by hand in dev.</b>
    </p>
    <aside class="notes">
        This is the strategy in one table. Recordings for the happy paths, short handwritten mocks for the failures, and a regular manual check against the real services, from a developer machine and never in CI, so the recordings do not lie for long.
    </aside>
</section>
